# BUG-265 regression: an `extern def` with NO C or library dependency must still
# take the LLVM backend, because the self-hosted one miscompiles a foreign call.
#
# THIS IS THE CASE ffi_lib_check.sh CANNOT COVER. That gate links a prebuilt
# library, and a non-empty `lib_sources` forces LLVM on its own — so it would keep
# passing even if the `emittedExtern()` half of the fix were deleted. The bug's
# actual shape is a program whose ONLY foreign dependency is a symbol resolved at
# link time, with nothing else to push it off the fast path. That is this file.
#
# WHY IT DISCRIMINATES (measured, not assumed). The same declaration built both
# ways, on identical source:
#     zig build-exe t.zig -lc                     -> abs=42
#     zig build-exe t.zig -fno-llvm -fno-lld      -> Segmentation fault
# So without the fix this fixture does not print 42 — it faults. The failure is at
# RUN time, which is why no compile-only gate can stand in for it: the emitted Zig
# is valid either way.
#
# `abs` rather than a Win32 export on purpose — it comes from libc, which the
# compiler already links with `-lc` on every program, so this fixture needs no
# sibling file and is not tied to Windows.

extern def abs(n: int32): int32

def main()
    print(abs(0 - 42))
